# The stdlib shim table builds a pylib call by NAME, via FindProc, which returns
# ONE proc index and never consults overloads
# (bug-nilpy-stdlib-shim-table-cannot-reach-an-overload).
#
# The dangerous half of that was not the missing arity — it was that the
# OBVIOUS FIX DID NOTHING. Adding a Pascal `overload` for the case the shim got
# wrong produced a clean build, a green self-host, and no behaviour change at
# all, so the next conclusion was that the runtime was wrong rather than that
# the call was never routed there. It cost three rediscoveries in one night, and
# os.path.join's 3- and 4-argument overloads were added, measured to be inert,
# and removed again.
#
# The shim call site now re-targets by ARITY (FindProcArity), so an ordinary
# same-named overload taking exactly this many arguments is reachable from a
# hand-built call. The `<name>_d` fallback is tried second, so the shims that
# already gave their two arities different names — os.environ.get(name) beside
# os.environ.get(name, default) — keep answering exactly as before.
#
# Type-based selection is still NOT reachable this way; that is stated at
# FindProc's declaration rather than left to be rediscovered a fourth time.
#
# Every expectation is CPython's.

import os
print(os.path.join("a", "b"))
print(os.path.join("a", "b", "c"))
print(os.path.join("a", "b", "c", "d"))
print(os.path.join("/x", "y", "z"))
print(os.path.join("a", "", "c"))

# the _d fallback, unchanged
print(os.environ.get("PXX_NOT_SET_XYZ", "dflt"))
